SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-only
Subject: [PATCH] net: phy: realtek: RTL8261C firmware: write every entry, load after every reset

The RTL8261C/CE driver (OpenWrt's backport of the v7.3 one) left the PHY with
a damaged firmware patch on the first start after boot, and with none at all
on every later one. On a W1700K2 against an RTL8159 adapter the first start
tried 10GBASE-T for about 80 s, then failed 5G and 2.5G three times each and
linked at 5 Gbit/s late or at 1 Gbit/s; taking the port down and up gave
5 Gbit/s in 20 s, which is the PHY without its patch.

- A write entry went through phy_modify_mmd(), which reads the register and
  leaves the write out when nothing would change. The patch's code is a run of
  words written to 0xa438, where each write takes the next address, so every
  word that matched what was read back was lost and the rest misplaced.
  Applied from user space the same way, the file leaves the PHY unable to
  link; applied with every write made, it links at 5 Gbit/s in 6 s (10G not
  advertised) or 19 s (three 10G attempts first), and repairs a PHY the driver
  had loaded.
- The firmware was loaded once per boot. A PHY with reset-gpios is taken
  through a hard reset whenever it is attached, which drops the patch
  (extension register 0x801e reads 0x0015 again, not 0x2215), so it is now
  loaded on every config_init.

--- a/drivers/net/phy/realtek/realtek_main.c
+++ b/drivers/net/phy/realtek/realtek_main.c
@@ -359,7 +359,6 @@
 
 struct rtl8261x_priv {
 	const char *fw_name;
-	bool fw_loaded;
 	/* LCR7's polarity bit doubles as the manual on/off level in
 	 * led_brightness_set(), so the configured polarity is kept here
 	 * to restore it whenever hardware control is set up again.
@@ -575,8 +574,23 @@
 
 	switch (entry->type) {
 	case OP_WRITE:
-		ret = phy_modify_mmd(phydev, dev, addr,
-				     GENMASK(msb, lsb), (value << lsb) & GENMASK(msb, lsb));
+		/* Every entry is written, also one whose value the register
+		 * already reads. The patch's code goes through 0xa438 as a
+		 * run of words, each write taking the next address:
+		 * phy_modify_mmd() leaves out a write that would not change
+		 * what it read back, and the words after one left out land in
+		 * the wrong place.
+		 */
+		bits = GENMASK(msb, lsb);
+		if (bits == 0xffff) {
+			ret = phy_write_mmd(phydev, dev, addr, value);
+		} else {
+			val = phy_read_mmd(phydev, dev, addr);
+			if (val < 0)
+				return val;
+			ret = phy_write_mmd(phydev, dev, addr,
+					    (val & ~bits) | ((value << lsb) & bits));
+		}
 		if (ret)
 			return ret;
 		break;
@@ -636,8 +650,6 @@
 		}
 	}
 
-	priv->fw_loaded = true;
-
 release_fw:
 	release_firmware(fw);
 	return ret;
@@ -851,10 +863,13 @@
 	int ret;
 
 	/* The firmware parameters are preserved across IEEE soft resets and
-	 * suspend/resume cycles. Reloading is only necessary after a power
-	 * cycle or hard reset.
+	 * suspend/resume cycles, and lost in a hard reset. A PHY with a reset
+	 * line is taken through one every time it is attached (phy_detach()
+	 * asserts the line, phy_init_hw() releases it before calling here),
+	 * so the firmware is loaded on every initialisation and not just on
+	 * the first; loading it over itself is harmless.
 	 */
-	if (priv->fw_name && !priv->fw_loaded) {
+	if (priv->fw_name) {
 		ret = rtl8261x_fw_load(phydev);
 		if (ret)
 			return ret;
